Content:
Asia's naphtha margin traded firm on Monday amid expectations of strong import demand from China even as supplies at the prompt remained abundant, analysts said.

The crack traded at about $129 per metric ton over Brent crude, compared with $132.65 on Friday. The second-half August cargo traded at $665.50 per ton.

Chinese refiners are expected to readjust product slate to crank up gasoline yields in anticipation of a relaxation in export curbs and reduce naphtha output at the same time, market participants said.

This will prop up import demand for naphtha, they added. Chinese buyers have surfaced in the market to snap up August supplies in recent weeks.

NEWS

- QatarEnergy has halved its scheduled deliveries of liquefied natural gas (LNG) to Bangladesh for this year, Petrobangla's acting chairman and industry sources said, as the fallout from the U.S.-Israeli war on Iran continues to curb shipments of the fuel through the Strait of Hormuz.

- Oil prices fell after OPEC+ agreed to further increase its output targets from August while exports from key producers via the Strait of Hormuz are recovering, potentially adding to global supplies.

