Content:
The latest projections from the National Weather Service are showing elevated temperatures spanning the U.S. Corn Belt while below-usual precipitation blankets most of the prime farming states in the middle of the country. The NWS's Climate Prediction Center says that below-normal rainfall is expected to be the case for a large area ranging from western North Dakota to central Tennessee--covering most Corn Belt states except for roughly half of Indiana and Ohio. This is expected to be seen through next week, according to the NWS. CBOT grain futures are up premarket on the latest forecasts, with corn rising 3.1%, soybeans climbing 3%, and wheat advancing 1.8%. (kirk.maltais@wsj.com)
