Content:
The Bank of Israel cut short-term interest rates for the second straight decision on Monday as inflation remained contained following Israel's war with Iran, helped by a near three-decade high in the shekel  against the dollar.

The central bank, as expected, reduced its benchmark rate (ILINR=ECI) to 3.5% from 3.75%. It had cut in November and January but paused in its two subsequent decisions due to the conflict with Iran and fears of a spike in supply-driven inflation. It resumed reductions in May.

The annual inflation rate held at 1.9% in May, well within a 1-3% target.
