Stock Symbol: ICEUS:DXY

Content:
Reversing some of the recent gains, the Indian rupee slipped below 95.38 against the US dollar today, a decline of around 0.2%, despite Brent Crude sliding to near $72 a barrel.

That's partly due to rising risk aversion in global markets that has pushed investors towards the US dollar. The Dollar Index, which measures the value of the American greenback against a basket of peer currencies, climbed back above 101 as global funds resumed their retreat from emerging markets, including India, commodities ($1.5 billion outflow), energy ($3.2 billion outflow), and other risk assets.

(The rupee's recent weakness has unfolded at an unprecedented pace. The latest move from 90 to 95 against the US dollar took only 152 days, compared with nearly five years for the 65–70 band)

India imports the bulk of its crude oil requirement, so lower oil prices typically provide relief to the rupee by easing pressure on the country's import bill. The recent correction in Brent crude should, in theory, have supported the currency. Instead, the rupee has remained under pressure even after oil retreated sharply from its US-Iran war highs. The reason lies beyond the oil market: the return of dollar strength and a renewed pullback in global capital flows.

Why is the dollar becoming stronger?

"This sets the stage for a halt in the Dollar Index's decline and a possible reversal in the next few months and quarters. The US Fed's reluctance to raise the Fed Funds rate can further put a floor under the US Dollar's decline. Dollar recessions can be negative for hard assets and emerging markets dependent on US imports," a report released on July 5 added.

Ever since the first monetary policy review under Kevin Warsh, the new chairman of the US Federal Reserve, the market has tilted towards expecting higher interest rates than it did before, leading to a strengthening of the US currency.

Data as of July 5, 2026

As the dollar outlook strengthened, gold funds saw their largest outflow in 14 weeks, with $3.1 billion exiting the asset class in just one week.

The broad consensus is that a reversal of the euphoria around AI stocks will shift some global fund flows towards India. However, the odds are more complicated than that.

While selling pressure on Indian equities has eased, well below the levels seen in March and April, when weekly outflows averaged nearly $1 billion, the decline in oil prices hasn't led to the inflows required to boost the value of the Indian rupee.

Redemptions from global emerging market funds have now extended into a ninth week, the longest streak of outflows since 2022-23, according to Elara Capital, a Mumbai-based broking firm.

The net impact was outflows from India

India recorded net outflows of $580 million during the week, a lot more than the $94 million a week earlier, according to an Elara Capital report based on EPFR data, which tracks capital flows from global mutual funds, exchange-traded funds, and closed-end funds.

Of the latest withdrawals, around $250 million came from India-dedicated funds, while the remainder was driven by selling from Global Emerging Market (GEM) and Asia ex-Japan (AXJ) allocations.

The shift is also becoming visible in developed markets. US equity funds recorded a second consecutive week of outflows, an uncommon occurrence that was last seen in January 2026 and February 2025.

Investors pulled $17 billion from US equities last week after redeeming $8 billion in the previous week, with exchange-traded funds accounting for $9 billion of the latest selling. That's in sharp contrast to the record $120 billion inflow just three weeks earlier. "The exceptional momentum in US allocations may be beginning to moderate," the Elara report said.

Fears of a reversal in the AI rally have hurt global risk appetite

Taiwan witnessed its largest foreign outflow in 11 years, with $766 million withdrawn, while Brazil remained the weakest performer after losing nearly $2 billion over the past seven weeks, wiping out all inflows recorded since April 2026.

However, the earnings growth estimate for India is nowhere near what markets expect from other AI destinations like South Korea.

Goldman Sachs' Asia strategist Timothy Moe expects South Korean companies to post around 320% earnings growth in 2026, valuing the market at 6.6 times forward earnings, the cheapest valuation since the 2008 global financial crisis.

That would explain, in part, why outflows from South Korea slowed to $283 million last week, despite a turn in the narrative around AI.

In comparison, the best estimates for Nifty 50 earnings for the financial year ending March 2027 are in the early teens.

What will make the rupee rise against the dollar?

Any positive signals from the upcoming earnings season, starting with Tata Consultancy Services (TCS) declaring its numbers on Thursday, may help. Globally, there are at least a couple of factors that may affect the trajectory of the US dollar this week: the US manufacturing PMI and the minutes of the last US Fed meeting.

The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has opened up the window for Indian banks to attract foreign currency deposits from non-resident Indians. The banks have attracted about $7 billion by the end of June, according to a Moneycontrol report. Many estimates see a total inflow of about $50 billion by September, and if that materialises, it may lift the rupee significantly.

To put it in perspective, foreign investors have withdrawn over $60 billion from Indian stocks in the last 21 months due to a sharp deceleration in corporate earnings growth, resulting in very high valuations.

Some believe it's just a matter of time before the dollars return to India. "The fall in commodity prices has altered the macro outlook for India almost overnight," said Sandip Sabharwal, founder of research house Asksandipsabharwal.com in Mumbai. "Lower commodity prices, improving capital flows and stable interest rates create an environment where earnings upgrades are likely to exceed downgrades over the coming quarters," he told Bloomberg.

The sell-off shrank India's premium over emerging markets in the MSCI EM Index to a historic low of 18% in June 2026, making it an attractive bet if earnings estimates improve. Foreign ownership of Indian equities fell to 14.2% at the end of June 2026, down from more than 20% a decade earlier.

Further, marquee initial public offerings (IPOs) from the likes of the National Stock Exchange (NSE), Jio Platforms, Zepto, and Walmart-owned PhonePe are likely to draw precious dollars.
