Content:
The Bank of Israel lowered its policy rate by 25 bps to 3.50% during its July 2026 meeting, as anticipated, delivering a second straight rate cut to the lowest level since 2022.

The decision was driven by stable inflation and easing geopolitical tensions following the signing of a memorandum of understanding between the US and Iran.

Policymakers noted that inflation remained around the midpoint of the target range in May, while Israel's risk premium returned to levels seen before October 2023.

Israel's annual inflation rate held at 1.9% in May 2026, unchanged from April and May and staying well within the Bank of Israel's 1%–3% target range.

The central bank said recent indicators point to a continued economic recovery following the slowdown at the start of the conflict with Iran.

The GDP is projected to grow 4.0% in 2026 and 5.5% in 2027, while the fiscal deficit is expected at 4.9% of GDP in 2026 and 4.2% in 2027, assuming no further increase in defense spending.
